WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.080
Proposition one asks whether California should borrow money for housing. A

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:09.640
bond supplies money now, while repayments and interest stretch across future

3
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:15.100
budgets. Some affordable housing already receives public grants or low-cost

4
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:20.500
loans. Developers combine that assistance with other financing, usually in

5
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:25.360
exchange for keeping homes affordable to eligible households. This measure

6
00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:30.680
authorizes eleven point two five billion dollars: ten billion for housing

7
00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:36.360
programs, and one point two five billion for veterans’ home loans. Those two

8
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.540
portions have different repayment sources.

9
00:00:39.233 --> 00:00:43.733
Imagine a renter looking for an affordable apartment. An apartment project

10
00:00:43.733 --> 00:00:48.053
might have land and a builder but still need cheaper financing to offer

11
00:00:48.053 --> 00:00:52.953
below-market rents. A public loan can help close that gap. This illustrates

12
00:00:52.953 --> 00:00:58.273
the mechanism, not a guarantee of a particular rent. A housing counselor

13
00:00:58.273 --> 00:01:00.313
explains the available programs.

14
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.100
The largest allocation is seven point two billion dollars to acquire, build,

15
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:10.900
or renovate multifamily rental housing. Projects generally reserve units for

16
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:15.340
lower-income households for fifty-five years. Other allocations support

17
00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:20.420
homeownership, infrastructure, farmworker housing, student housing, and

18
00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:25.520
tribal housing. Eligibility depends on the particular program; passing the

19
00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:29.640
bond does not give every applicant a home. The analyst estimates subsidies

20
00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.780
for up to forty thousand multifamily units. That includes housing acquired

21
00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:39.600
or renovated, so it is not a promise of forty thousand additional newly

22
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.587
built homes. The General Fund would repay the housing portion at about five

23
00:01:44.587 --> 00:01:50.100
hundred to six hundred million dollars a year for roughly twenty-five years.

24
00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.120
Veterans’ loan payments would repay their separate bond portion.

25
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:58.400
A voter might support financing affordable homes sooner rather than waiting

26
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.960
for enough money in annual budgets. Supporting construction and preserving

27
00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:07.880
restricted rents can help specific households even when the broader market

28
00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:09.180
remains expensive.

29
00:02:09.633 --> 00:02:14.073
You might ask whether the long commitment is worthwhile. A skeptical voter

30
00:02:14.073 --> 00:02:19.013
could prefer paying as funds become available, or addressing permitting and

31
00:02:19.013 --> 00:02:24.293
construction costs first. Interest adds expense, and future repayments

32
00:02:24.293 --> 00:02:29.373
compete with other services. Money alone does not remove every obstacle to

33
00:02:29.373 --> 00:02:29.693
building.

34
00:02:30.233 --> 00:02:35.953
In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 55 percent said yes, 43

35
00:02:35.953 --> 00:02:41.773
percent no, and 2 percent were unsure. These are dated survey results, not

36
00:02:41.773 --> 00:02:42.913
votes already cast.

37
00:02:43.533 --> 00:02:48.713
Yes authorizes this borrowing; no rejects this bond, while existing housing

38
00:02:48.713 --> 00:02:53.653
programs continue. Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source

39
00:02:53.653 --> 00:02:58.533
links, and official voter guide. Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own

40
00:02:58.533 --> 00:02:59.593
informed choice.
